  "question": {
    "text": "Hydrogen (<sub>1</sub>H<sup>1</sup>), Deuterium (<sub>1</sub>H<sup>2</sup>), singly ionised Helium (<sub>2</sub>He<sup>4</sup>)<sup>+</sup> and doubly ionised lithium (<sub>3</sub>Li<sup>6</sup>)<sup>++</sup> all have one electron around the nucleus. Consider an electron transition from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>. If the wave lengths of emitted radiation are <math> <mrow> <msub> <mi>&#955;</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> <mtext>,&#160;</mtext> <msub> <mi>&#955;</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mtext>,&#160;</mtext> <msub> <mi>&#955;</mi> <mn>3</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math>&nbsp;and <math> <mrow> <msub> <mi>&#955;</mi> <mn>4</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math>&nbsp;respectively then approximately which one of the following is correct ?",
    "image": null
  },
